  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nCurrent satellite imagery shows a moisture boundary progressing \nNW over EP with building clouds south of the metro area. CAMs, \nparticularly the HREF, are showing isolated showers developing \nalong this boundary overnight (1-3am), pushing northeastward \nthrough the lower valley/Horizon City area. Rain amounts should be\nlight (0.1-0.25\"), but high-end rainfall of 1\" is possible based \non the HREF. The REFS is much more bearish on rain chances (~10%) \nand amounts in east El Paso overnight. \n\nClearing is forecast by sunrise with high low-level humidity and \nlight winds resulting in patchy fog east of the RGV. For Tue, \nzonal flow takes over, pushing much of the moisture eastward and \nending storm chances other than along the Hudspeth/Culberson line.\nWinds become modestly breezy from the SW in the afternoon for \nnorthern areas. Similar wx is forecast on Wed as the flow aloft \nturns SW, starting to pull moisture northward out of Mexico. \n\nMoisture advection intensifies through Thu as a shortwave trough \napproaches and provides forcing for ascent. This disturbance will \nresult in scattered to widespread showers and embedded t-storms \nThu afternoon and night, especially E of the RGV. With modest \ninstability and weak shear, severe storms are not anticipated from\nthis system. However, there is a low risk of flash flooding due \nto repeated showers/storms. 90th %-ile NBM shows 1.5-2.5\" E of US \n54 through Thu night. The flood threat and rain chances will lower\nas you head west. A weaker shortwave moves in from the SW for \nFri, bringing better rain chances to western areas. For Fri, the \nflood threat favors the Gila Region, but lower overall compared to\nThu. Zonal flow and drier air returns on Sat. Seasonal temps, \nhigher clouds, and generally light winds are forecast for \nHalloween.\n\nThe next trough arrives for Sun, bringing more clouds and rain \nchances Sun/Mon. NM will be favored for rain early next week with \ndrier conditions in far west TX. Western areas and the high \nterrain are most likely to see flash flooding, but confidence is \nlow. Breezy winds should accompany the trough/frontal passage on \nSun. Temps will be near normal through midweek, then cooling a bit\nearly next week behind the Pacific front.",
